.Dd September 30, 2026
.Dt NOVI-NOTIFY 1
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm novi-notify
.Nd send a desktop notification
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.Nm
.Op Fl u Ar urgency
.Op Fl i Ar icon
.Ar summary
.Op Ar body
.Sh DESCRIPTION
.Nm
hands a short message to
.Nm novi-notifyd ,
which draws it on the desktop, and
.Sy always
writes it to the system log as well.
On a console-only machine that log line is the whole feature, which
is why the sender is in the base image and the daemon is a package:
the things with something to say
.Pf ( Xr novi-mount 8 ,
.Xr novi-eject 1 )
are base tools that run on machines with no desktop.
.Bl -tag -width "-u urgency"
.It Fl u Ar urgency
.Cm low ,
.Cm normal
(the default) or
.Cm critical .
.Cm critical
.Sy never expires :
something that matters enough to be called critical should not
vanish while the person is looking away.
Use it sparingly.
.It Fl i Ar icon
An icon name.
A name this build does not know draws
.Em no icon ,
never a fallback and never an error.
.El
.Ss Everything here is untrusted text
The transport is a world-writable
.Ux Ns -domain
datagram socket by design: an unprivileged program has as much
business notifying as root does, and there is no session bus to
arbitrate.
The largest sender announces a volume by its filesystem label, which
is a string off a stranger's device.
So control characters are
.Sy dropped
rather than escaped, lengths are capped, and urgency and icon are
matched against fixed lists.
.Pp
It is a datagram socket and specifically not a FIFO:
open(2)
for writing on a FIFO with no reader blocks forever, and the largest
caller is a uevent handler, where that would stop the kernel's
hotplug queue.
.Sh FILES
.Bl -tag -width "/run/novi/notifications" -compact
.It Pa /run/novi/notify.sock
The socket.
.It Pa /run/novi/notifications
The last fifty, republished by the daemon.
.El
.Sh EXIT STATUS
.Ex -std
Sending to a socket nobody is listening on is not an error: the log
line still went out.
.Sh EXAMPLES
.Bd -literal -offset indent
$ novi-notify "Backup finished" "412 files, 2.1 GB"
$ novi-notify -u critical -i drive "Volume removed while mounted"
.Ed
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr novi-eject 1 ,
.Xr novi-mount 8
.Sh STANDARDS
RFC 0024, RFC 0034.
